From 963931aab737dafddfbeb80d42d67aaf8da49258 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 20:45:57 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm: leave native Dolby Vision on nvidia-drm with
 the change of mode that follows, in one commit

Leaving native Dolby Vision on nvidia-drm turns the head off and on
again in the mode of the video (so that NVKMS brings the head and the
link up from scratch), and when the video has stopped Kodi sets the
mode of the GUI right after it: two changes of the HDMI link in a row.

In the tests of 2026-10-14 (RTX 4080 SUPER, LG C5, the GUI in 2160p at
119.88 Hz, which needs HDMI FRL; the first time 2160p at 60 Hz with 12
bits, the same) the change to the GUI mode took 2.5 to 3.8 s after a
Dolby Vision video, against 1.3 s after an HDR10 one. Twice NVKMS waited
more than its 3 s for the display engine ("Lost display notification",
nvkms-dma.c), and from then on no flip came and the head could not be
set again ("Failed to apply atomic modeset", -22): the TV stayed without
a picture until the PC was switched off. Every end of an HDR10 video,
one change of the output, went well in every test.

So, on nvidia-drm, when a video whose output was native Dolby Vision
stops and Kodi is going to set another mode of the size of the window
(the resolution it goes back to when a video stops, as
CGraphicContext::SetFullScreenVideo() decides it, unless the refresh
rate is adjusted at the start only), the restoration waits for that
change of mode, 2 s at most, with the last frame on the display, and is
committed with the new mode: one change of the output, as at the end of
an HDR10 video. The new mode is another one, so the head is set up from
scratch anyway. Where no such change comes, the output is restored as
before (off and on, in the mode of the video); where that commit does
not go through, the output is turned off and on in the new mode, and if
nothing goes through the mode of the video is selected again. Nothing
changes where the mode stays (the refresh rate is not adjusted), where
the GUI has another size, while the display is off, or on the kernel
backends (i915, amdgpu).

A restoration that ends the wait puts back, after the output state of
before the video, the colorimetry and the HDR metadata that Kodi asked
for since: the renderer that went cleared them while it waited (before,
they went in the commit after the restoration). The window keeps its
surface meanwhile (SetVideoOutput() of the renderer that goes restores
at once only if the surface would change, and the EGL config in use is
not touched to find that out), and it is not destroyed with a
restoration still to be made: Kodi stopping during a Dolby Vision video
(quit, restart, power off) restores first. Captures do not read the
transport framebuffer while it waits.
